From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] drm: allwinner: A733 DP -- the transmitter becomes a PHY consumer and configures the main link

[Recipe cut: regenerated: the debugfs knobs removed from the AUX patch are
not touched here.]

This is the caller `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-rate-2700`'s rate table never had. The DisplayPort transmitter takes
the COMB0 combo-PHY as a phy consumer and asks it for a link rate and a lane
count through phy_configure() with a union phy_configure_opts_dp -- on the
shipping driver, in a kernel with no bring-up Kconfig set.

WHY THE TRANSMITTER AND NOT THE PHY ITSELF
------------------------------------------

The alternative was for the combo-PHY to apply its own rate table when the
Type-C mux selects the DisplayPort personality, i.e. inside `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-register-a-typec-mux` and `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-reset-sequence`. It was
rejected, and the evidence is not a preference:

  1. THE RATE IS NOT KNOWN AT ALT-MODE ENTRY. It is the outcome of reading the
     sink's DPCD (0x0001 MAX_LINK_RATE, 0x0002 MAX_LANE_COUNT) over AUX and
     intersecting it with the source. The DP alt-mode VDO carries a SIGNALLING
     field, which is a capability, not a negotiated link rate. The mux callback
     has no AUX channel and no DPCD; this driver owns both.
  2. RATE AND LANE FALLBACK ARE TRAINING DECISIONS. DP 1.4 falls back after a
     failed clock recovery or channel equalisation -- events only the link
     training state machine sees. A mux callback fires once, at entry, and
     never hears that training failed.
  3. SWING AND PRE-EMPHASIS ARRIVE PER LANE FROM THE SINK. DPCD 0x206/0x207
     ADJUST_REQUEST is answered inside the training loop, through the same
     phy_configure() with set_voltages. There is no version of the mux-side
     design that can deliver those, so it would have to be split anyway --
     leaving one path for the rate and another for the swing, on the same PMA.
  4. IT IS WHAT THE FRAMEWORK IS FOR. include/linux/phy/phy-dp.h defines
     phy_configure_opts_dp with link_rate, lanes, voltage[4], pre[4], ssc and
     the four set_* flags -- a DP controller's vocabulary, not a Type-C mux's.
     phy_ops.configure is "used to change the PHY parameters", and phy_ops.validate
     exists so a consumer can ask before changing them.
  5. IT IS WHAT MAINLINE DOES, WITHOUT EXCEPTION. Every DisplayPort controller in
     the tree that has a serdes behind it is the phy_configure() caller:
     drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/synopsys/dw-dp.c:596-660,
     drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/cadence/cdns-mhdp8546-core.c:808-1130 (which is the
     consumer of phy-cadence-torrent.c -- and our PHY is a Torrent derivative),
     drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dp/dp_ctrl.c:1298-1943,
     drivers/gpu/drm/mediatek/mtk_dp.c:1256-1273,
     drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/analogix/analogix_dp_reg.c:524-601,
     drivers/gpu/drm/xlnx/zynqmp_dp.c:738-746.
     Zero PHY drivers apply a DP rate table from a Type-C mux callback. The
     closest thing in mainline to our hardware -- phy-qcom-qmp-combo.c, a
     USB3+DP combo PHY with a Type-C mux on the same lanes -- splits it exactly
     this way: qmp_combo_typec_mux_set() (:3808) chooses the personality and
     touches no rate table, while the rate tables live behind
     qmp_combo_dp_configure() (:2971) and are applied from the DP consumer's
     request.
  6. IT IS ALSO WHAT THE VENDOR DOES. Its eDP driver takes the combo-PHY with
     devm_phy_get(dev, "combo-phy") and calls phy_configure() from its rate/lane
     setup and again from its training loop
     (bsp/drivers/drm/sunxi_device/sunxi_edp.c:604-641, sunxi_drm_edp.c:2906).
     On this one point the vendor and mainline agree, and so do we.

WHAT THIS DRIVER NOW DOES
-------------------------

  - takes the main-link PHY with devm_phy_optional_get(dev, "dp"). The DT side is
    `arm64-dts-a733-dp-main-link-phy`.
  - reads the sink's own capability bytes with drm_dp_read_dpcd_caps() before
    choosing anything. The rate and the lane count come from the sink, not from a
    constant.
  - picks the highest rate the sink offers that the PHY validates, clamping the
    lane count to the four COMB0 physically has.
  - takes phy_init() on that PHY -- refcounted, so it never pulls COMB0 out from
    under dwc3 -- because phy.h says phy_ops.configure requires it.
  - calls phy_configure() once, from one function, and CHECKS THE RETURN.
  - exposes `link` under debugfs: read for the sink's caps and what the PHY
    accepted, write "<rate Mb/s> [lanes]" for an exact request or "0" for the
    best the two agree on.

ONE CONSEQUENCE OF THE PHANDLE, NAMED RATHER THAN DISCOVERED
------------------------------------------------------------

Taking the phy properly means this driver's probe now depends on combophy0's.
devm_phy_optional_get() returns -EPROBE_DEFER while that provider has not
registered, and dev_err_probe() passes it up, so sun60i-dp defers -- correctly --
until COMB0 exists. The consequence is that if the combo-PHY never probes at all,
the AUX channel is unreachable too, where before it was not.

That is the right trade and it is deliberate: combophy0 probing is already a
prerequisite for the SuperSpeed link this board is proven on, and the alternative
is the vendor's -- swallow the deferral, come up with no main link, and say so
only at INFO level. A driver that half-probes is harder to diagnose than one that
defers. But it is a change in what a combo-PHY probe failure costs, so it is
written down here rather than found later.

WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO
--------------------------------

  - It does not write DPCD 0x100 LINK_BW_SET / 0x101 LANE_COUNT_SET, nor this
    core's own link registers. Those two ends must be set together with the
    training pattern sequence; writing one of them here would leave source and
    sink describing different links with nothing in the tree to re-converge
    them -- which is exactly the failure seen in an earlier test on the board,
    where only LINK_BW_SET and DPCD 0x100 moved and the PMA stayed at
    2.7 Gbps.
  - It does not train. No training pattern, no DPCD 0x102, no adjust-request
    loop. Link training, including where it will differ from the vendor's
    training path, is owed by the training patch, and this patch does not
    pretend to have paid it.
  - It does not call phy_power_on()/phy_power_off() on the combo-PHY. Which
    personality COMB0 carries is the Type-C mux's decision, driven by TCPM from
    the alt-mode negotiation. A transmitter that powered the lanes up on its own
    would be racing that decision -- and the PHY refuses a configure until the
    lanes really are DisplayPort's, so it could not win the race anyway.
  - It does not configure anything at bind: nothing here runs until the
    AUX channel has been opened deliberately.

WHY A DEBUGFS FILE IS THE TRIGGER, AND HOW IT DIFFERS FROM DEBUG INSTRUMENTATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

`drm-allwinner-a733-displayport-aux` established the pattern and the reason has not changed: this tree has no KMS
driver, no drm_device and no HPD path into one, so there is nothing else to call
sun60i_dp_link_configure(). What matters is the difference from a DP
debug-instrumentation patch that this recipe does not carry, which is what this
patch exists to get away from:

  - That patch is CONFIG_PHY_SUN60I_A733_COMBO_DP_BRINGUP, bool, default n. It does not
    exist in a delivery kernel. This is compiled into the shipping module.
  - It is an actuator: it drives the Type-C switch and mux callbacks by hand
    and takes the four lanes away from SuperSpeed by construction. This takes
    nothing: it configures a PHY that has already been given to DisplayPort, and
    the PHY refuses it otherwise.
  - sun60i_dp_link_configure() is shaped after dw_dp_link_configure() /
    dw_dp_phy_configure() (dw-dp.c:596-660) precisely so that link training calls
    THIS function and adds its DPCD writes around it, rather than growing a
    second way to reach the PHY.

WHERE THE VENDOR DRIVER DIFFERS, AND WHAT THIS PATCH DOES
---------------------------------------------------------

The vendor's shape on this path is right -- its DP driver
is a phy consumer -- and this tree says so rather than inventing a difference.
Its handling differs:

| vendor | ours |
|---|---|
| devm_phy_get(dev, "combo-phy") run through IS_ERR_OR_NULL() and turned into "not setting, maybe not used" (sunxi_drm_edp.c:2906-2911): -EPROBE_DEFER is swallowed, so on a boot where the combo-PHY has not probed yet the DP driver comes up permanently with no main link and logs it at INFO | devm_phy_optional_get(), which returns NULL only for an ABSENT property and propagates every real error including the deferral, through dev_err_probe() |
| phy_configure() called from two void helpers whose return value is never looked at (sunxi_edp.c:604-641) -- a PHY that refused a rate is indistinguishable from one that applied it | the return is checked, logged with the rate and lane count that were refused, and the recorded link state is cleared rather than left claiming a rate |
| phy_validate()'s answer mapped so that both -EOPNOTSUPP and -EINVAL become "pass" (sunxi_drm_edp.c:846-868), so the one case its PHY does reject is discarded; and when two phys are present the second overwrites the first's answer | validate is used to CHOOSE, not to approve: a rate the PHY does not validate is skipped. The -EOPNOTSUPP ambiguity (no .validate op vs a provider refusing with that code) is resolved by falling through to the sink maximum and letting configure give the real answer -- never by treating a refusal as a pass |
| never calls phy_init() on the combo-PHY -- edp_phy_init() inits aux_phy and dp_phy only, and edp_phy_exit() carries "// ignore combo phy, leave it until other module configure it" (sunxi_drm_edp.c:826-843), i.e. it depends on the USB stack having initialised the PHY and says so | phy_init()/phy_exit() taken and released by this driver, refcounted by the framework, so the dependency is expressed instead of assumed |
| the link rate is taken from a lane_para structure filled elsewhere; there is no rate or lane fallback anywhere in the path | the rate comes from the sink's DPCD each time the link is configured, and the ladder that will carry fallback is here and consults the PHY |

WHAT IT OWES
------------

  1. The four-lane result reproduced on a kernel WITHOUT
     CONFIG_PHY_SUN60I_A733_COMBO_DP_BRINGUP. Observable, with a sink on J4 and
     the port in a DP-only pin assignment:

       echo 1 > /sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/aux_enable
       cat     /sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/link      # sink caps
       echo 0 >/sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/link      # configure
       cat     /sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/link      # 2700 Mb/s, 4 lanes

     then train by hand over AUX and read DPCD
     0x202/0x203 = 0x77 0x77, 0x204 bit0, 0x205 bit0, and 0x210.. = 00 80 per
     lane. Falsified by: `link` reporting a configured rate while PMA idx 0xC010
     still reads 0x5100 -- that would mean the call returned success without the
     table landing.
  2. The negative control, which is the half that matters: with NO DP pin
     assignment entered, `echo 0 > link` must FAIL, with the PHY's own message
     about the lanes not being DisplayPort's, and LINK_CTRL0 0x06C01000 must
     still read 0x00000111.
  3. A SUPERSPEED RE-PROOF. This node now references the combophy0 node that is
     HW-proven at 10 Gbps, and RST_BUS_SERDES is shared. Observable: with no DP
     sink attached, `lsusb -t` still shows a SuperSpeed device at 10000M after a
     full boot, and after `echo 1 > aux_enable` as well -- the AUX path takes no
     reference on the main-link PHY.
  4. DPCD is read over AUX every time the link is configured, so a sink that
     NACKs will fail the configure rather than produce a guessed rate. Not yet
     observed on hardware.

Not hardware-proven, checked only by an automated build. No kernel was built
locally for this patch by design; it applies at fuzz 0 on the full series.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
@@ -4,11 +4,19 @@
  *
  * The A733's DisplayPort transmitter is a Trilinear DP 1.4 core at 0x05740000,
  * fed by TCON_TV1 and driving the SERDES COMB0 lanes out of the Type-C
- * connector. This driver implements exactly one part of it -- the AUX channel --
- * and deliberately nothing else: no link training, no video, no KMS. AUX is what
- * carries DPCD and EDID, so it is the first piece whose result can be checked
- * against something the sink says about itself rather than against a register we
- * wrote ourselves.
+ * connector. This driver implements two parts of it and deliberately nothing
+ * else: the AUX channel, and the configuration of the main link's PHY.
+ *
+ * AUX is what carries DPCD and EDID, so it was the first piece whose result can
+ * be checked against something the sink says about itself rather than against a
+ * register we wrote ourselves.
+ *
+ * The main link is the second, and it is a PHY consumer's job: COMB0 belongs to
+ * the combo-PHY driver, and a DisplayPort controller reaches it through
+ * phy_configure() with a union phy_configure_opts_dp, the way every DP
+ * controller in mainline does. What is still absent is link training and video:
+ * no training pattern sequence, no DPCD 0x100/0x102 writes, no mode setting, no
+ * KMS.
  *
  * Every register offset and field below comes from the UM's own register list
  * (chapter 15.4.7 of the A733 user manual) and
@@ -37,6 +45,7 @@
  * Copyright (c) 2026 DockSeed
  */
 
+#include <linux/array_size.h>
 #include <linux/bitfield.h>
 #include <linux/clk.h>
 #include <linux/debugfs.h>
@@ -44,6 +53,7 @@
 #include <linux/i2c.h>
 #include <linux/io.h>
 #include <linux/iopoll.h>
+#include <linux/minmax.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/mutex.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
@@ -51,6 +61,8 @@
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
 #include <linux/reset.h>
 #include <linux/seq_file.h>
+#include <linux/sprintf.h>
+#include <linux/uaccess.h>
 
 #include <drm/display/drm_dp.h>
 #include <drm/display/drm_dp_helper.h>
@@ -105,15 +117,51 @@
 
 #define AUX_MAX_PAYLOAD			16
 
+/*
+ * COMB0 carries four lanes to the Type-C connector J4 and there is no fifth lane
+ *, so a
+ * sink advertising more is clamped rather than believed.
+ */
+#define SUN60I_DP_MAX_LANES		4
+
+/*
+ * The DP link rate ladder, highest first, in the units DRM counts link rates in
+ * -- drm_dp_bw_code_to_link_rate() returns 162000 for RBR, so the unit is 10 kHz
+ * and Mb/s is that divided by 100. dw-dp.c:604 makes the same conversion for the
+ * same reason: phy_configure_opts_dp counts in Mb/s.
+ *
+ * The ladder exists so that rate selection can ask the PHY what it can do
+ * instead of assuming. Today the PHY has one table (2700 Mb/s), so "auto" lands
+ * there; when a second table is ported, this code needs no change.
+ */
+static const unsigned int sun60i_dp_link_rates[] = { 810000, 540000, 270000, 162000 };
+
+#define SUN60I_DP_RATE_TO_MBPS(r)	((unsigned int)(r) / 100)
+
 struct sun60i_dp {
 	struct device		*dev;
 	void __iomem		*base;
 	struct clk		*bus_clk;
 	struct reset_control	*reset;
 	struct phy		*aux_phy;
+	/* The main link. A second consumer of the COMB0 combo-PHY, which dwc3
+	 * also holds -- the two are mutually exclusive on the wire and the
+	 * Type-C mux arbitrates, so one struct phy with two consumers is what
+	 * the hardware is. This driver never powers it on or off; it only
+	 * configures it.
+	 */
+	struct phy		*link_phy;
+	bool			link_phy_inited;
 	struct drm_dp_aux	aux;
 	struct mutex		lock;		/* serialises enable/disable */
 	bool			enabled;
+	/* The sink's own capability bytes, and what was last asked of the PHY.
+	 * rate is in Mb/s, the unit phy_configure_opts_dp counts in.
+	 */
+	u8			dpcd[DP_RECEIVER_CAP_SIZE];
+	bool			dpcd_valid;
+	unsigned int		link_rate;
+	unsigned int		link_lanes;
 };
 
 static inline struct sun60i_dp *aux_to_dp(struct drm_dp_aux *aux)
@@ -334,16 +382,225 @@ static int sun60i_dp_aux_enable(struct sun60i_dp *dp)
 	return ret;
 }
 
+static void sun60i_dp_link_release(struct sun60i_dp *dp)
+{
+	if (!dp->link_phy_inited)
+		return;
+
+	/* Refcounted: the combo-PHY's own .exit runs only when the LAST
+	 * consumer leaves, so this never pulls COMB0 out from under dwc3
+	 * (drivers/phy/phy-core.c, phy_exit()).
+	 */
+	phy_exit(dp->link_phy);
+	dp->link_phy_inited = false;
+	dp->link_rate = 0;
+	dp->link_lanes = 0;
+}
+
 static void sun60i_dp_aux_disable(struct sun60i_dp *dp)
 {
+	sun60i_dp_link_release(dp);
 	i2c_del_adapter(&dp->aux.ddc);
 	phy_power_off(dp->aux_phy);
 	phy_exit(dp->aux_phy);
 	reset_control_assert(dp->reset);
 	clk_disable_unprepare(dp->bus_clk);
+	dp->dpcd_valid = false;
 	dp->enabled = false;
 }
 
+/*
+ * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+ * The main link
+ * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+ *
+ * COMB0's four lanes are configured through the generic PHY framework, exactly
+ * as every DisplayPort controller in mainline configures its serdes:
+ * phy_configure() with a union phy_configure_opts_dp. The transmitter is the
+ * consumer and the combo-PHY is the provider; nothing else in this tree calls
+ * phy_configure() on the real path, which is why four lanes did not train.
+ *
+ * This driver never calls phy_power_on()/phy_power_off() on that PHY. Which
+ * personality COMB0 carries is the Type-C mux's decision, driven by TCPM from
+ * the alt-mode negotiation, and the PHY refuses a configure until the lanes are
+ * actually DisplayPort's. A transmitter that powered the lanes up on its own
+ * would be racing that decision.
+ */
+
+/* Fill the DP half of a configure/validate request. The union is zeroed first
+ * and always: its callers in mainline routinely leave it partly uninitialised
+ * (dw-dp.c declares it on the stack and sets only the fields it uses), and a provider that
+ * logs or bounds-checks a field the consumer never set then reads stack.
+ */
+static void sun60i_dp_fill_opts(union phy_configure_opts *opts,
+				unsigned int rate_mbps, unsigned int lanes)
+{
+	*opts = (union phy_configure_opts){
+		.dp = {
+			.link_rate = rate_mbps,
+			.lanes	   = lanes,
+			.set_rate  = 1,
+			.set_lanes = 1,
+		},
+	};
+}
+
+/*
+ * Read what the sink says it can do. This is the sink's own answer, not a
+ * register of ours reading back, which is why the rate and lane count are taken
+ * from here rather than assumed.
+ */
+static int sun60i_dp_link_probe(struct sun60i_dp *dp)
+{
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = drm_dp_read_dpcd_caps(&dp->aux, dp->dpcd);
+	if (ret < 0) {
+		dev_err(dp->dev, "DPCD capability read failed: %d\n", ret);
+		dp->dpcd_valid = false;
+		return ret;
+	}
+
+	dp->dpcd_valid = true;
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Pick the highest rate the sink offers that the PHY says it can produce.
+ *
+ * phy_validate() is what makes this a question rather than a guess, and it has
+ * one ambiguity worth naming: phy_core returns -EOPNOTSUPP both for "this PHY
+ * has no .validate op" and for a provider that refuses the request with that
+ * code. So a ladder that finds nothing is NOT treated as "no rate works" -- it
+ * drops back to the sink maximum and lets phy_configure() give the real
+ * answer. The vendor resolves the same ambiguity the other way and it costs it
+ * the check: sunxi_drm_edp.c:846-868 maps both -EOPNOTSUPP and -EINVAL to
+ * "pass", so a PHY that rejects a lane count is indistinguishable from one that
+ * has no opinion.
+ */
+static unsigned int sun60i_dp_link_best_rate(struct sun60i_dp *dp,
+					     unsigned int lanes)
+{
+	unsigned int sink_max = drm_dp_max_link_rate(dp->dpcd);
+	union phy_configure_opts opts;
+	unsigned int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(sun60i_dp_link_rates); i++) {
+		unsigned int mbps;
+
+		if (sun60i_dp_link_rates[i] > sink_max)
+			continue;
+
+		mbps = SUN60I_DP_RATE_TO_MBPS(sun60i_dp_link_rates[i]);
+		sun60i_dp_fill_opts(&opts, mbps, lanes);
+
+		if (!phy_validate(dp->link_phy, PHY_MODE_DP, PHY_SUBMODE_DP,
+				  &opts))
+			return mbps;
+	}
+
+	dev_warn(dp->dev,
+		 "no link rate up to %u Mb/s validated by the PHY; asking for the sink maximum anyway\n",
+		 SUN60I_DP_RATE_TO_MBPS(sink_max));
+
+	return SUN60I_DP_RATE_TO_MBPS(sink_max);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Configure the main link. THIS is the function link training calls -- it is
+ * shaped after dw_dp_link_configure()/dw_dp_phy_configure()
+ * (drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/synopsys/dw-dp.c:596-660) so that when training lands
+ * it calls this and adds its own DPCD writes, rather than growing a second way
+ * to reach the PHY.
+ *
+ * rate_mbps == 0 or lanes == 0 mean "the best this sink and this PHY agree on".
+ *
+ * What this deliberately does NOT do: write DPCD 0x100/0x101 LINK_BW_SET and
+ * LANE_COUNT_SET, or this core's own link registers. Those two must be set
+ * together with the training pattern sequence, and writing one end here would
+ * leave the source and the sink describing different links with nothing in the
+ * tree to re-converge them -- which is the exact failure mode seen in an earlier
+ * test on the board, when only LINK_BW_SET moved.
+ */
+static int sun60i_dp_link_configure(struct sun60i_dp *dp, unsigned int rate_mbps,
+				    unsigned int lanes)
+{
+	union phy_configure_opts opts;
+	int ret;
+
+	if (!dp->enabled) {
+		dev_err(dp->dev, "the AUX channel is down; nothing can be read from the sink\n");
+		return -ENODEV;
+	}
+
+	if (!dp->link_phy) {
+		dev_err(dp->dev,
+			"no main-link PHY: this node needs phys = <&combophy0> with phy-names \"dp\"\n");
+		return -ENODEV;
+	}
+
+	ret = sun60i_dp_link_probe(dp);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	if (!lanes)
+		lanes = drm_dp_max_lane_count(dp->dpcd);
+	lanes = min_t(unsigned int, lanes, SUN60I_DP_MAX_LANES);
+
+	/* A sink that answers 0 here is answered by name rather than passed
+	 * down to be refused with less context.
+	 */
+	if (!lanes) {
+		dev_err(dp->dev,
+			"sink reports 0 lanes (DPCD 0x0002 = 0x%02x); nothing to configure\n",
+			dp->dpcd[DP_MAX_LANE_COUNT]);
+		return -EINVAL;
+	}
+
+	if (!rate_mbps)
+		rate_mbps = sun60i_dp_link_best_rate(dp, lanes);
+
+	/* phy_ops.configure requires phy_init() to have been called
+	 * (include/linux/phy/phy.h). Taken here rather than at bind, so a board
+	 * that never uses DisplayPort never initialises COMB0 on this driver's
+	 * account, and released again in sun60i_dp_link_release().
+	 */
+	if (!dp->link_phy_inited) {
+		ret = phy_init(dp->link_phy);
+		if (ret) {
+			dev_err(dp->dev, "main-link phy_init: %d\n", ret);
+			return ret;
+		}
+		dp->link_phy_inited = true;
+	}
+
+	sun60i_dp_fill_opts(&opts, rate_mbps, lanes);
+
+	ret = phy_configure(dp->link_phy, &opts);
+	if (ret) {
+		/* Checked, not discarded. The vendor calls phy_configure() from
+		 * two void helpers and looks at neither return value
+		 * (sunxi_edp.c:604-641), so a PHY that refused a rate is
+		 * indistinguishable there from one that applied it.
+		 */
+		dev_err(dp->dev,
+			"main-link PHY refused %u Mb/s over %u lanes: %d\n",
+			rate_mbps, lanes, ret);
+		dp->link_rate = 0;
+		dp->link_lanes = 0;
+		return ret;
+	}
+
+	dp->link_rate = rate_mbps;
+	dp->link_lanes = lanes;
+
+	dev_info(dp->dev, "main link configured: %u Mb/s, %u lanes\n",
+		 rate_mbps, lanes);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
 static void sun60i_dp_teardown(void *data)
 {
 	struct sun60i_dp *dp = data;
@@ -391,6 +648,24 @@ static int sun60i_dp_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	if (IS_ERR(dp->aux_phy))
